/*!

\mainpage Introduction to CARTESIANGRIDPLUGIN

\b CARTESIANGRIDPLUGIN is a plug-in for the SALOME Mesh module (SMESH). It fills a geometry with a regular 3D grid of
boxes, aligned with the X, Y and Z axes. Each box of the grid is called a <b>cell</b>, and each cell becomes a
hexahedron (a mesh element with 8 corners) in the mesh.

The plug-in is useful when a simulation code (a "solver") works on a Cartesian grid and needs to know which material
fills each cell. Besides building an ordinary SMESH mesh, the plug-in can write the grid and the material of every cell
to a compact MED file, without building the mesh at all.

The plug-in adds two things to the Mesh module:
- a 3D meshing algorithm called <b>Cartesian Grid 3D</b>;
- its settings, a hypothesis called <b>Cartesian Grid Parameters</b>, which defines where the grid lines are.

<h2>How a cell is given to a solid</h2>

The geometry is made of one or more solids. The plug-in does not cut cells along the surfaces of the solids. Instead,
it looks only at the <b>center</b> of each cell and asks: which solid contains this point?

\image html cartesiangrid_what.png "(a) The geometry: one solid, seen here in a cut by a horizontal plane. (b) The grid placed over the solid. Only the center of each cell is tested (dots): a filled dot is inside the solid, an empty dot is outside. (c) The cells whose center is inside the solid become hexahedra of that solid." width=760px

As picture (c) shows, the result follows the shape of the solid in steps, like a staircase: a curved surface becomes a
set of small flat steps. No cell is cut into a smaller piece. The finer the grid, the smaller the steps.

When the center of a cell is inside several solids at once, the cell goes to the <b>smallest</b> of these solids (the
one with the smallest volume). A center lying exactly on the surface of a solid counts as inside it. If two solids have
exactly the same volume, the one that comes first in the geometry wins.

A cell whose center is in no solid at all is removed from the mesh. You can choose to keep these cells instead (option
\b Keep in the hypothesis); they are then attached to the whole geometry rather than to one solid.

<h2>Why the smallest solid wins</h2>

Many geometries are built by placing volumes inside other volumes, without cutting the inner volume out of the outer
one. This way of describing a geometry is common in CSG modelling (constructive solid geometry).

Here is an example. A large box of AIR contains a box of IRON, and the IRON box contains a disc of LEAD. The IRON box
is not cut out of the AIR box: the AIR solid is still a complete box. In the same way, the IRON solid is still a
complete box, even where the LEAD disc sits inside it. The three solids overlap. The modeller simply means "where an
inner volume is placed, it replaces the volume around it". This is a convention for reading the geometry; the solids
themselves do not show it.

Most meshers cannot work with overlapping solids: they need solids that touch each other without overlapping. The Body
Fitting algorithm of SMESH, for example, fails on such a geometry. To use these meshers, you would first have to cut
the solids with boolean operations (Partition, Cut). These operations create new solids, so any group defined on the
original solids (for example one group per material) would have to be rebuilt. Boolean operations also take time and
can fail on complex CAD models.

The "smallest solid wins" rule gives the right answer without any boolean operation. A point inside the LEAD disc is
also inside the IRON box and inside the AIR box. Among these three solids, LEAD is the smallest, so the cell goes to
LEAD. An inner volume is always smaller than the volume that contains it, so the innermost volume always wins.

\image html cartesiangrid_cut.png "(a) Uncut geometry: three complete solids overlap. AIR (the largest) contains IRON, and IRON contains LEAD (the smallest). (b) The same materials after boolean cuts: three separate solids that only touch each other. (c) The cells are the same in both cases. In (a), a center inside the lead disc is inside all three solids and goes to LEAD, the smallest one. In (b), it is inside LEAD only." width=760px

<h2>It also works on geometry that is already cut</h2>

The rule does not require overlapping solids; it only decides what happens when solids overlap. If your geometry is
already cut into separate solids (for example a Partition made in the Geometry module, or a CAD assembly whose parts
share faces), each cell center is inside exactly one solid, and that solid gets the cell.

There is one special case: a cell center lying exactly on a face shared by two solids. The center then counts as inside
both solids, and the smaller solid gets the cell (or the first one in the geometry if both have the same volume). The
answer is always the same for the same geometry, and no cell is lost or counted twice.

So the same algorithm works on both kinds of geometry, and on a mix of the two: some volumes cut, others simply placed
inside another one. The result depends only on which material is found at each cell center, not on how the CAD model
was built.

<b>A limit of the rule.</b> When two solids overlap only partly, without one being inside the other (for example two
boxes that cross each other), the overlapping part goes to the smaller solid. This may not be what the modeller
intended. CSG descriptions usually forbid this kind of overlap (GDML does, for example), so in practice it only
happens with geometry built by hand. Overlapping solids are accepted everywhere in the plug-in; the only thing the
Cartesian MED export refuses is a solid that belongs to two zone groups.

<h2>From solids to materials</h2>

To get one material per cell, create groups of solids in the geometry, one group per material. In SHAPER, when a GDML
file is imported, the command <i>Create material groups</i> does this for you: it creates one group named
<code>MAT_&lt;material&gt;</code> for each material, each group listing the solids made of that material.

These groups are then used in two ways:
- After a compute, check <b>Create all groups on geometry</b> in the <b>Create Mesh</b> dialog (or let the wizard do
  it). SMESH then creates one mesh group per geometry group, holding the hexahedra of that group's solids.
- The \ref cartesiangridplugin_export_page "Cartesian MED export" uses the groups directly: it writes the material of
  every cell into the MED file, without creating any mesh element.

<h2>Pages of this guide</h2>

- \subpage cartesiangridplugin_hypo_page "Cartesian Grid Parameters hypothesis": how to place the grid lines along
  each axis, and what to do with the cells outside the solids.
- \subpage cartesiangridplugin_wizard_page "Cartesian Grid Wizard": a single dialog that goes from a geometry to the
  Cartesian MED file.
- \subpage cartesiangridplugin_export_page "Cartesian MED export": what the MED file contains, and how a solver can
  read it.
- \subpage cartesiangridplugin_algorithm_page "How the algorithm works": the steps of a compute, the fast cell
  classification, and timings.
- \subpage cartesiangridplugin_python_interface_page "Python interface": how to do all of this from a script.

<h2>Limits</h2>

- The algorithm must be assigned to the whole geometry (the main shape), so that it sees all the solids. Sub-meshes are
  not supported.
- Every solid must be closed (a real volume, with no hole in its surface). A solid smaller than a cell may get no cell
  at all; you then get a warning.
- The plug-in sets no limit on the number of cells; the memory of your computer does. A computed SMESH mesh needs about
  760 bytes per hexahedron (nodes included), so 10<sup>8</sup> cells need about 76 GB. The Cartesian MED export alone
  needs only about 16 bytes per cell. Along one axis, the grid may have at most 2<sup>31</sup>-2 cells. Before
  computing, the \b Evaluate command of SMESH gives the exact number of nodes and hexahedra.
- Most of the compute time is spent creating the SMESH nodes and hexahedra (about 80 %). This part runs on one
  processor core only, because the SMESH data structure cannot be filled by several threads at once. Finding the solid
  of each cell runs on all cores.
- In rare cases, the geometry kernel cannot tell whether a cell center is inside a solid. The cell is then considered
  outside that solid, and a warning gives the number of such cells.

The dialogs, the wizard, its summary and its own error messages follow the language chosen in SALOME (English or
French). As in the other SMESH plug-ins, the error messages that come from the meshing engine itself (compute errors,
grid errors, export errors) are always in English.

*/
